      ],
      "children": [
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "sc1r",
          "text": "Colorado ski law is primarily in the ",
          "marks": []
        },
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "sc1t",
          "text": "Ski Safety Act of 1979",
          "marks": [
            "lc1s"
          ]
        },
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "sc1u",
          "text": " and within the Colorado Revised Statutes §33-44-100. The Act includes thirteen sections covering the duties of skiers and operators, limitations of liability, and the types of claims that a skier may bring for injuries.",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "_type": "block",
      "_key": "bc1x",
      "style": "normal",
      "markDefs": [],
      "children": [
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "sc1w",
          "text": "Generally, a skier or snowboarder can obtain compensation from another skier or snowboarder resulting from a collision. The uphill skier or rider has a duty to maintain a safe distance behind a downhill skier. Therefore, the uphill skier is presumed liable in a collision with another skier or snowboarder.",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "_type": "block",
      "_key": "bc1z",
      "style": "normal",
      "markDefs": [],
      "children": [
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "sc1y",
          "text": "Collision claims depend heavily upon witnesses and available objective data. Sources of ski data include:",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "_type": "block",
      "_key": "bc21",
      "style": "normal",
      "markDefs": [],
      "children": [
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "sc20",
          "text": "Ski pass logs tracking the times and number of runs a skier makes in a day",
